-- thinadhoo-gaafu-dhaalu
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Thinadhoo Island?","answer":"The most convenient way to reach Thinadhoo is by domestic flight from Male to Kaadedhdhoo Airport (1 hour 15 minutes), followed by a 30-minute speedboat transfer. Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 18 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 8 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Thinadhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Thinadhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year, with excellent visibility for snorkeling and diving."},{"question":"What makes Thinadhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Thinadhoo stands out as the administrative capital of Gaafu Dhaalu Atoll, offering a unique blend of traditional island life and modern conveniences. Unlike more remote islands, Thinadhoo has well-developed infrastructure while maintaining its authentic character. The island provides excellent access to the southern atolls'' pristine marine environments with fewer visitors than northern areas."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Thinadhoo Island?","answer":"As Thinadhoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities or at resort day visits."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Thinadhoo?","answer":"Thinadhoo''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish. The southern atolls are known for their excellent visibility and less disturbed marine environments compared to more visited northern areas."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'thinadhoo-gaafu-dhaalu';


