-- thoddoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Thoddoo Island from Male?","answer":"You can reach Thoddoo Island from Male by public ferry or speedboat. The public ferry operates three times a week (Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday), departing from Male at 9:00 AM and arriving at Thoddoo around 1:00 PM. Some guesthouses also offer private transfers for a higher fee. It''s recommended to book your transfers in advance, especially during high season."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Thoddoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Thoddoo Island depends on your priorities. The northeast monsoon (December-April) generally provides the best overall weather conditions with less rainfall, lower humidity, and calmer seas, making it ideal for beach activities and water sports. This is also when the island''s watermelon production is at its peak. The southwest monsoon (May-November) may have occasional rain showers but still offers many sunny days, fewer tourists, and lower prices. For agricultural enthusiasts, different fruits are in season throughout the year, so there''s always something interesting to see in the farms. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) year-round."},{"question":"What makes Thoddoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Thoddoo stands out from other local islands in the Maldives primarily because of its agricultural focus. While most Maldivian islands rely on fishing and tourism, Thoddoo is famous for its fruit production, particularly watermelons, papayas, and bananas. The island''s unique position as a separate island outside the main atoll ring gives it exceptional beaches on all sides. Thoddoo also has a distinctive cultural identity centered around agriculture, with farming traditions passed down through generations. Visitors to Thoddoo can enjoy both the typical Maldivian attractions (beautiful beaches, snorkeling, water activities) and unique agricultural experiences like farm tours and fruit tastings that aren''t available on other islands."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Thoddoo Island?","answer":"Yes, as Thoddoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas. Both men and women should cover shoulders and knees when walking around the village, visiting shops, or dining in local restaurants. Swimwear (including bikinis) is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach\" area on the northern side of the island. When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads with a scarf. These dress code requirements are a sign of respect for local culture and traditions and are generally well-enforced on the island."},{"question":"Can I buy fresh fruits directly from farms on Thoddoo?","answer":"Yes, one of the highlights of visiting Thoddoo is the opportunity to purchase and taste fresh fruits directly from local farms. Many farmers welcome visitors and sell their produce on-site, offering the freshest fruits possible. Some guesthouses can arrange farm visits that include fruit tasting and purchasing opportunities. There are also small fruit stands in the village where farmers sell their daily harvest. The island is particularly famous for its sweet watermelons, papayas, and bananas, but depending on the season, you might find various other tropical fruits as well. Prices are generally very reasonable compared to what you would pay at resorts or in Male."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'thoddoo';


-- thulhaadhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Thulhaadhoo Island?","answer":"Thulhaadhoo Island is accessible via several transportation options. The most convenient route is by domestic flight from Velana International Airport to Dharavandhoo Airport (approximately 30 minutes), followed by a 25-minute speedboat transfer to Thulhaadhoo. Island hopping is another option, with connections available from Eydhafushi (the atoll capital) via local boats. Most guesthouses on Thulhaadhoo can arrange transfers and will provide detailed information about the best options based on your arrival date and preferences. It''s advisable to plan your transportation well in advance, especially during high season (November to April)."},{"question":"What is the traditional lacquerware craft and why is it significant?","answer":"Traditional Maldivian lacquerware (liyelaa jehun) is an ancient craft that involves creating decorative items from wood, which are then coated with layers of resin and natural pigments and etched with intricate designs. The process begins with carving wooden objects like boxes, vases, or containers from specific types of wood. These are then smoothed and coated with a base layer of black resin derived from natural sources. Multiple layers of colored resin are applied, each requiring drying time. The distinctive designs are created by carefully etching through the layers to reveal the colors beneath, followed by polishing to create a smooth, glossy finish. This craft is significant because it represents one of the oldest continuous artistic traditions in the Maldives, dating back centuries. Historically, lacquerware items were used in royal courts and by nobility, and they held both practical and ceremonial importance. Today, Thulhaadhoo is one of the last places where this traditional craft is still practiced using authentic methods and materials, making it a living repository of this important aspect of Maldivian cultural heritage. The craft connects present-day Maldivians with their cultural past and provides a unique artistic expression that distinguishes the Maldives from other cultures in the region."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Thulhaadhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Thulhaadhoo Island depends on your interests. For general beach activities, cultural experiences, and snorkeling, the northeast monsoon season (November to April) brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. January to March typically offers the most reliable weather conditions with minimal rainfall. If you''re specifically interested in manta ray and whale shark encounters at nearby Hanifaru Bay, plan your visit between May and November, with peak season being June to October when plankton concentrations attract these magnificent creatures. The southwest monsoon season (May to October) brings occasional rain showers and slightly rougher seas, though there are still many sunny days. This period offers advantages including fewer tourists, lower prices, and the chance to witness the spectacular marine life aggregations in the UNESCO Biosphere Reserve. For cultural experiences, the island hosts various festivals throughout the year, with Islamic holidays like Eid being particularly festive times to visit. The lacquerware craft can be observed year-round, as it is not seasonal. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year, making Thulhaadhoo a viable destination in any season depending on your preferences."},{"question":"What makes Thulhaadhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Thulhaadhoo distinguishes itself from other local islands through several unique characteristics. First and foremost is its status as the center of traditional lacquerware craftsmanship in the Maldives. This ancient art form has been preserved on Thulhaadhoo when it has largely disappeared from other islands, creating a living cultural heritage that visitors can experience firsthand. Second, the island has a larger population than many local islands, resulting in a more vibrant community with diverse activities and cultural expressions. Third, Thulhaadhoo''s location within the Baa Atoll UNESCO Biosphere Reserve provides excellent access to marine highlights like Hanifaru Bay while maintaining an authentic atmosphere. Fourth, the island has developed a craft-based tourism model that focuses on cultural exchange and artistic traditions rather than just beach holidays. This approach attracts visitors interested in meaningful cultural immersion. Fifth, the island''s relatively larger size offers more extensive beaches and varied landscapes compared to some smaller local islands. For travelers seeking to experience both unique cultural heritage and natural beauty, Thulhaadhoo offers a distinctive combination that sets it apart from other destinations in the Maldives."},{"question":"What souvenirs should I consider purchasing from Thulhaadhoo?","answer":"Thulhaadhoo offers several unique souvenirs that showcase the island''s cultural heritage and craftsmanship. The most distinctive souvenirs are authentic lacquerware pieces created by local artisans. These include decorative boxes (malafai) in various sizes, which make excellent keepsakes or jewelry containers. Traditional water containers (dhaani) represent historical household items with beautiful decorative patterns. Lacquerware plates, vases, and bowls combine functionality with artistic beauty. Smaller items like lacquerware jewelry, ornaments, or keychains offer more affordable options while still featuring the distinctive craftsmanship. Beyond lacquerware, consider traditional woven mats (thundu kunaa) with geometric patterns, handmade coir rope products, or miniature traditional dhoni (boat) models crafted by local woodworkers. When purchasing souvenirs, buy directly from artisans or local craft shops to ensure authenticity and that your purchase supports the local community. Remember that each lacquerware piece is handmade using traditional techniques, making every item unique. The price reflects the considerable skill, time, and materials involved in creating these pieces. These souvenirs not only provide beautiful mementos of your visit but also help preserve important cultural traditions and support local artisans."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'thulhaadhoo';


-- thulusdhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Kaafu Atoll, North Male Atoll"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 1,500 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.2 km long, 0.6 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"28 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Public ferry, speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Cokes surf break, Coca-Cola factory, pristine beaches"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Surfing, budget-friendly stays, authentic island experience"}],"island_overview":["Located in the Kaafu Atoll just 28 kilometers northeast of Male, Thulusdhoo Island offers visitors an authentic Maldivian experience with the perfect blend of local culture and natural beauty. This vibrant island is easily accessible yet maintains its genuine island character, making it an ideal destination for travelers seeking both adventure and relaxation.","What makes Thulusdhoo special is its world-renowned surfing spots and unique industrial heritage. The island is home to the famous \"Cokes\" surf break, named after the Coca-Cola factory that has operated on the island since 1980 - the only one in the Maldives. This combination of excellent surfing conditions and cultural significance has created a distinctive identity that sets Thulusdhoo apart from other islands in the archipelago.","Visitors to Thulusdhoo are drawn by its excellent surfing opportunities, pristine beaches, exceptional snorkeling, and chances to engage with local community life. The island''s relatively close proximity to Male means easier access than more remote islands, while still offering an authentic experience away from the luxury resorts that dominate many parts of the Maldives."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo Island is situated in the northeastern part of Kaafu Atoll (North Male Atoll), approximately 28 kilometers from Male. This strategic location provides relatively easy access while still offering a genuine local island experience away from the capital''s urban environment.","Its position at the edge of North Male Atoll exposes it to excellent ocean swells, creating the perfect conditions for the world-class surf breaks that have made the island famous among surfers. The surrounding waters feature vibrant coral reefs and abundant marine life, ideal for snorkeling and diving enthusiasts."]},{"heading":"Thulusdhoo: Surfing Paradise & Coca-Cola Island","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo Island stands out among Maldivian islands for its world-class surfing conditions and unique industrial heritage, offering visitors a distinctive blend of adventure sports and cultural significance."]},{"heading":"World-Class Surfing","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo is renowned for its two famous surf breaks: \"Cokes\" on the eastern side (named after the Coca-Cola factory) and \"Chickens\" on the western side, attracting surfers from around the world with consistent waves and perfect conditions."]},{"heading":"Coca-Cola Factory","paragraphs":["The island is home to the only Coca-Cola bottling plant in the Maldives, operating since 1980, which has given Thulusdhoo its nickname \"Coca-Cola Island\" and created a unique industrial heritage that complements its natural beauty."]},{"heading":"Natural Beauty","paragraphs":["Beyond its surfing and industrial significance, Thulusdhoo boasts pristine white sand beaches with crystal-clear waters, offering the classic Maldivian beach experience with a more relaxed, budget-friendly atmosphere."]},{"heading":"Island Community","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo is home to a vibrant community that has embraced tourism while maintaining traditional island values, offering visitors authentic cultural interactions in a more accessible location than many other local islands."]},{"heading":"Surfing Paradise","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo''s surfing heritage includes several distinctive elements:"]},{"heading":"Coca-Cola Heritage","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo''s unique industrial identity includes:"]},{"heading":"Beaches & Marine Life","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo is blessed with beautiful beaches and a spectacular marine environment. The island''s location at the edge of North Male Atoll provides access to healthy coral reefs and abundant marine life, offering excellent snorkeling and diving opportunities."]},{"heading":"Beautiful Beaches","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo features several stunning beaches with soft white sand and crystal-clear turquoise waters. The main beach on the eastern side (near Cokes surf break) offers beautiful views and good swimming conditions when the surf is calm. The northern beach, often called \"Bikini Beach,\" is designated for tourists and allows Western-style swimwear, providing a relaxing environment while respecting local cultural norms."]},{"heading":"Bikini Beach","paragraphs":["The island''s designated \"bikini beach\" on the northern shore is a beautiful stretch of sand where tourists can wear Western-style swimwear. This area has been developed with visitors in mind, featuring beach chairs, umbrellas, and sometimes small beach bars serving non-alcoholic beverages. The remaining beaches require modest dress as they are used by the local community."]},{"heading":"Surf Viewpoints","paragraphs":["Unique to Thulusdhoo are the excellent viewpoints along the eastern shore where visitors can watch surfers tackling the famous Cokes break. These areas provide fascinating insights into the surf culture that has become an integral part of the island''s identity, with opportunities to observe world-class surfing against the backdrop of stunning ocean vistas."]},{"heading":"Marine Highlights","paragraphs":["The waters around Thulusdhoo Island offer excellent marine biodiversity:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo offers a diverse range of activities centered around its surfing culture and natural environment. The island''s unique combination of world-class waves, beautiful beaches, and cultural attractions provides visitors with plenty of options for both adventure and relaxation."]},{"heading":"Surfing","paragraphs":["Experience world-class waves at the famous Cokes and Chickens surf breaks, with lessons and equipment rental available for all skill levels."]},{"heading":"Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore vibrant house reefs directly from shore or join boat excursions to nearby reef systems."]},{"heading":"Coca-Cola Factory Visit","paragraphs":["Arrange a visit to the island''s famous Coca-Cola bottling plant, the only one in the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Sandbank Excursions","paragraphs":["Visit pristine uninhabited sandbanks for picnics, swimming, and the ultimate desert island experience."]},{"heading":"Night Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience traditional night fishing with local fishermen, using hand lines under the stars."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping","paragraphs":["Visit neighboring islands in Kaafu Atoll to experience different communities and environments."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo is home to a vibrant community of approximately 1,500 residents who have adapted to tourism while maintaining their traditional values and way of life. The island''s population is known for its warm hospitality, entrepreneurial spirit, and preservation of authentic Maldivian culture."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Thulusdhoo revolves around a blend of traditional activities, tourism services, and industrial work. The island awakens with the call to prayer, followed by a day of work, family responsibilities, and community interactions. Fishing remains important to the local economy, alongside employment at the Coca-Cola factory and various tourism-related businesses. The surf culture has influenced island life, with many locals becoming skilled surfers themselves and working as surf guides or instructors."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Thulusdhoo preserves many traditional Maldivian customs and cultural practices. Religious observances play an important role in community life, with festivals like Eid celebrated enthusiastically. Cultural expressions such as Boduberu performances remain vibrant, while traditional practices including fishing methods and local cuisine are still maintained by community members."]},{"heading":"Surf & Industrial Identity","paragraphs":["What makes Thulusdhoo''s community special is their unique blend of surf culture and industrial heritage. The presence of world-class surf breaks has attracted international visitors for decades, leading many locals to embrace surfing and related businesses. Meanwhile, the Coca-Cola factory has provided stable employment and given the island its distinctive nickname."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Thulusdhoo Island?","answer":"Thulusdhoo is one of the more accessible local islands in the Maldives. You have several transportation options: 1) Public Ferry: The most economical option is the public ferry from Male, which operates several times a week (usually Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday) and takes about 90 minutes. 2) Speedboat: For more convenience, speedboat transfers are available daily from Male or directly from Velana International Airport. Most guesthouses can arrange these transfers for you."},{"question":"When is the best time to surf at Thulusdhoo?","answer":"The prime surfing season at Thulusdhoo runs from April to October, with the peak months being June, July, and August when the southwest monsoon brings consistent swells to the eastern side of North Male Atoll. During this period, the famous Cokes surf break receives clean, powerful waves ranging from 3 to 8 feet (1-2.5 meters), creating perfect right-hand barrels over the reef. Chickens, the break on the western side of the island, works best during the northeast monsoon (November to March) when winds shift direction."},{"question":"Can I visit the Coca-Cola factory on Thulusdhoo?","answer":"Visiting the Coca-Cola factory on Thulusdhoo is possible but requires some advance planning. The factory is not officially open for regular tourist visits as it''s a working production facility. However, informal tours can sometimes be arranged through your guesthouse or local contacts. These visits are subject to production schedules and management approval, so they cannot be guaranteed."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Thulusdhoo?","answer":"Thulusdhoo offers a diverse range of accommodation options to suit different budgets and preferences. The island has developed a good tourism infrastructure with numerous guesthouses and small hotels, particularly catering to the surfing community. Surf-focused guesthouses are popular, offering amenities like board storage, equipment rental, and surf guides. Several boutique hotels provide higher-end accommodations with more amenities, stylish rooms, quality dining options, and additional services like spa treatments."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["himmafushi","huraa","dhiffushi","maafushi","gulhi","guraidhoo","hulhumale","male","kaashidhoo","gaafaru"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'thulusdhoo';


-- thuraakunu
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Thuraakunu Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 16 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 8 hours from Hanimaadhoo)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Thuraakunu Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Thuraakunu Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. The southwest monsoon (May to November) can bring rougher seas but offers excellent fishing opportunities. Water temperature remains warm year-round."},{"question":"What makes Thuraakunu different from other Maldivian islands?","answer":"Thuraakunu stands out as the northernmost inhabited island in the Maldives, offering a truly remote experience. Its isolation has preserved traditional Maldivian culture to a greater degree than more accessible islands. The marine life here includes species not commonly seen in central atolls, and the island''s geographical position creates unique weather and ocean conditions."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Thuraakunu Island?","answer":"As Thuraakunu is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas (shoulders and knees covered). Swimwear is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach.\" When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads. These requirements don''t apply during water activities."},{"question":"What facilities are available on Thuraakunu Island?","answer":"Thuraakunu has basic facilities including a small health center, school, and mosque. There are a few small shops selling essentials. Accommodations are simple guesthouses with basic amenities. There are no ATMs, so bring sufficient cash. Electricity may be generator-dependent in some properties. Wi-Fi access is limited."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'thuraakunu';


-- ukulhas
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Ukulhas Island from Male?","answer":"You can reach Ukulhas Island from Male by public ferry or speedboat. The public ferry operates three times a week (Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday), departing from Male at 9:00 AM and arriving at Ukulhas around 12:30 PM. Some guesthouses also offer private transfers for a higher fee. It''s recommended to book your transfers in advance, especially during high season."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Ukulhas Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Ukulhas Island is during the northeast monsoon (December-April), which generally provides the best overall weather conditions with less rainfall, lower humidity, and calmer seas. This period is ideal for snorkeling, diving, and beach activities. The water visibility is excellent during these months, making it perfect for marine life observation. The southwest monsoon (May-November) may have occasional rain showers but still offers many sunny days, fewer tourists, and lower prices. For manta ray sightings, the period from November to April is best. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) year-round."},{"question":"What makes Ukulhas different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Ukulhas stands out from other local islands in the Maldives primarily because of its pioneering environmental initiatives. It was the first inhabited island in the Maldives to implement a systematic waste management program and has received the Green Leaf Award for its environmental protection efforts. The island is exceptionally clean and well-maintained compared to many other local islands. Ukulhas also boasts particularly beautiful beaches and an exceptionally healthy house reef accessible directly from shore. The community''s collective commitment to sustainability creates a unique atmosphere where environmental consciousness is integrated into daily life and tourism activities."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Ukulhas Island?","answer":"Yes, as Ukulhas is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas. Both men and women should cover shoulders and knees when walking around the village, visiting shops, or dining in local restaurants. Swimwear (including bikinis) is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach\" area on the eastern side of the island. When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads with a scarf. These dress code requirements are a sign of respect for local culture and traditions and are generally well-enforced on the island."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Ukulhas?","answer":"Ukulhas is renowned for its exceptional marine biodiversity, and snorkelers can expect to see a wide variety of underwater life directly from the beach. Common sightings include colorful reef fish, moray eels, octopuses, reef sharks (particularly blacktip reef sharks), sea turtles (both green and hawksbill), eagle rays, and various species of rays. During certain seasons (typically November to April), manta rays can be spotted at nearby cleaning stations. The house reef features healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. For the best snorkeling experience, ask your guesthouse about the current conditions and best entry points, as these can change with tides and seasons."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'ukulhas';


-- uligamu
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Uligamu Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 16-18 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses. As a port of entry, Uligamu can also be accessed directly by private vessels arriving from international waters."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Uligamu Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Uligamu Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. The northern atolls can experience slightly different weather patterns than the southern regions, but generally follow similar seasonal trends. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Uligamu different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Uligamu stands out for its strategic position as the northernmost inhabited island in the Maldives and its role as a port of entry. Its natural harbor has made it an important gateway for vessels arriving from India and Sri Lanka, with customs and immigration facilities. Despite this role, Uligamu maintains the tranquil atmosphere and authentic culture that characterize Maldivian local islands, offering visitors a unique blend of strategic importance and traditional island life."},{"question":"Can I see vessels from different countries at Uligamu''s harbor?","answer":"Yes, as a port of entry, Uligamu''s natural harbor often hosts vessels from different countries, particularly those arriving from India and Sri Lanka. The harbor serves as a point of entry for international vessels entering Maldivian waters, where they complete customs and immigration formalities. Visitors can observe various types of boats, from traditional fishing dhonis to international yachts, and learn about the maritime activities that have shaped the island''s development."},{"question":"What excursions can I take from Uligamu Island?","answer":"From Uligamu, visitors can enjoy a range of excursions including snorkeling trips to nearby reefs, visits to uninhabited islands, traditional fishing experiences, and island hopping to other inhabited islands in Haa Alifu Atoll. Unique to Uligamu are harbor tours to learn about its role as a port of entry, visits to the northernmost point of the Maldives, and opportunities to observe traditional boat building and repair activities. Most guesthouses can arrange these excursions, often sharing boats with other visitors to make them more affordable."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'uligamu';


-- ungoofaaru
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Raa Atoll, Northern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 2,800 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"2.1 km long, 1.2 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"162 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Seaplane or domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Magnificent culture, stunning beaches, excellent snorkeling"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Culture enthusiasts, beach lovers, snorkelers, adventure seekers"}],"island_overview":["Ungoofaaru Island is a magnificent local island in Raa Atoll that offers visitors a remarkable glimpse into traditional Maldivian life. This outstanding island combines natural beauty with a splendid atmosphere, providing an ideal destination for travelers seeking exceptional cultural experiences and stunning tropical surroundings.","Known for its beautiful beaches, magnificent coral reefs, and excellent marine biodiversity, Ungoofaaru provides wonderful opportunities for snorkeling, swimming, and underwater exploration. The island''s remarkable community embraces visitors with traditional Maldivian warmth and shares their outstanding way of life with magnificent hospitality.","What makes Ungoofaaru special is its magnificent natural environment and remarkable island setting. Visitors can enjoy stunning beaches, explore rich marine ecosystems, and experience outstanding island culture while staying in comfortable accommodations that showcase the magnificent Maldivian lifestyle."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru Island is beautifully located in the northern part of Raa Atoll, approximately 162 kilometers from Male. This location provides a magnificent and remarkable experience while maintaining good accessibility to the capital and international airport through various transfer options.","The island''s position offers excellent protection from strong currents while providing access to stunning coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems. Its location in the atoll makes it an ideal base for exploring the natural beauty and cultural richness of Raa Atoll in its most magnificent form."]},{"heading":"Ungoofaaru: Magnificent Paradise & Marine Splendor","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru Island is blessed with exceptional natural beauty, featuring stunning white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and one of the most magnificent marine environments in Raa Atoll. The island offers perfect conditions for beach relaxation and marine exploration in a remarkable, outstanding setting."]},{"heading":"Stunning White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru features magnificent white sand beaches with gentle slopes and calm waters, perfect for swimming, sunbathing, and remarkable beach activities."]},{"heading":"Magnificent House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from the beach and offers outstanding snorkeling with diverse marine life and colorful coral formations."]},{"heading":"Remarkable Island Community","paragraphs":["Experience the outstanding spirit of traditional island life with friendly locals and magnificent Maldivian culture in a remarkable community setting."]},{"heading":"Outstanding Sandbank Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks and stunning islands around Ungoofaaru for magnificent tropical paradise experiences with rich marine life."]},{"heading":"Beach Features","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru''s coastal highlights include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Ungoofaaru feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru offers a perfect blend of marine adventures, cultural experiences, and magnificent activities. The island''s remarkable environment and outstanding atmosphere provide ideal conditions for various activities in a stunning, splendid setting."]},{"heading":"Magnificent House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the outstanding house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility in magnificent waters."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby stunning dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses in magnificent marine environments."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, stunning sandbanks, and discover the natural beauty of Raa Atoll with outstanding exploration opportunities."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips in magnificent community settings."]},{"heading":"Outstanding Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience magnificent Maldivian island life with remarkable cultural exchanges."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy stunning sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing in magnificent waters."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru is home to approximately 2,800 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and magnificent cultural traditions that create a remarkable atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Ungoofaaru follows traditional patterns centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains a magnificent atmosphere where visitors can experience outstanding Maldivian culture and customs in their most remarkable, splendid setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this magnificent island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["Ungoofaaru has embraced sustainable tourism practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and magnificent cultural heritage for future generations to experience and enjoy."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Ungoofaaru Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Raa Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Ungoofaaru?","answer":"The best time to visit Ungoofaaru is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more magnificent local atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Ungoofaaru?","answer":"Ungoofaaru offers various accommodations including magnificent guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a remarkable setting."},{"question":"What makes Ungoofaaru''s community special?","answer":"Ungoofaaru is known for its magnificent and remarkable community atmosphere. The island maintains traditional Maldivian cultural practices while offering an outstanding environment perfect for cultural exploration. Visitors can experience exceptional cultural exchanges and participate in magnificent community activities."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["rasgetheemu","rasmaadhoo","vaadhoo","alifushi","angolhitheemu","dhuvaafaru","fainu","hulhudhuffaaru","inguraidhoo","innamaadhoo","kinolhas","maakurathu","maduvvaree","meedhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'ungoofaaru';


